#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087452 :question/id #uuid "1e3c0253-226d-596c-a831-7c68ec76b21c" :question/math-academy-id "e-21741" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 2y = 2e^{-2x}\n$$\n\nWrite the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x) \\right) = I(x)Q(x),$ where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor." :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 2$ and $Q(x) = 2e^{-2x}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = e^{2x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x} \\\\ {e}^{2x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{e}^{2x}\\cdot y & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y I(x)\\right){:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{2x}) & ={e}^{2x}\\cdot 2{e}^{-2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087453 :question/id #uuid "89446fd7-75c2-5da5-bd97-469100f3ca7b" :question/math-academy-id "e-21742" :question/problem "Find the general solution of the following differential equation using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + \\dfrac {3} {x} y = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}, \\qquad x \\gt 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x)\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = \\dfrac{3}{x}$ and $Q(x) = \\dfrac{\\cos x}{x^2}.$ We proceed to find an integrating factor $I(x)$ using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int (3/x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})} \\\\  & ={x}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply our differential equation through by $I(x) = x^3$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{3}{x}y) & ={x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}\\cdot y & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the left hand side as $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}\\left(y\\cdot I(x)\\right)$ and carry out the integration:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3}) & =x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{x}^{3})\\text{d}x & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this integral, we integrate by parts. \n\n$$\n\\int uv'\\,\\textrm d x = uv - \\int u' v\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nLet $u = x, u' = 1, v' = \\cos x,$ and $v = \\sin x.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y{x}^{3} & =\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{x}^{3} & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{C}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087454 :question/id #uuid "09d09b0b-2132-59b2-9eb9-ae0748f09252" :question/math-academy-id "e-21743" :question/problem "Solve the following initial value problem using the method of integrating factors.\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x} + 3x^2y = 9x^2, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$" :question/worked-solution "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} + P(x)y = Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x) = 3x^2$ and $Q(x) = 9x^2.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & ={e}^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{\\int 3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x) = e^{x^3}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}y) & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot 9{x}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\cdot y & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}}) & =9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x,$ making use of the substitution $u = x^3{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(y{e}^{{x}^{3}})\\text{d}x & =\\int 9{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =\\int 3{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{u}+C \\\\ y{e}^{{x}^{3}} & =3{e}^{{x}^{3}}+C \\\\ y & =3+C{e}^{-{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0) = 1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =3+C{e}^{-{0}^{3}} \\\\ 1 & =3+C \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 3 - 2e^{-x^3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088059 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abc22f1d-811f-5bdc-b37d-0282e04c8aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(yx) = x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085089} {:answer/id #uuid "da5a2b38-cb05-5079-8a67-44b3023a6a33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(y\\ln x) = \\ln x\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085090} {:answer/id #uuid "011ca4e6-3122-58c6-a064-72bf61a7cc03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx^{2}}(yx^{2}) = x^{2}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085091} {:answer/id #uuid "5dc23e01-156d-58f7-838b-e84160a81d5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}\\left(\\frac{y}{x}\\right) = \\frac{\\cos x}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085092} {:answer/id #uuid "6436279c-38d0-5c1d-a595-b7ab9c80521e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{d}{dx}(ye^{x}) = e^{x}\\cos x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085093}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b1c0749d-dee0-5f31-b150-55643e10de7e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085088}] :question/id #uuid "c7fefc62-82f5-5578-9142-a780040dc0af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17540" :question/problem "Consider the following first-order linear ODE for the function $y = y(x)$:\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + y = \\cos x$\nWrite the equation in the form $\\frac{d}{dx}(yI(x)) = I(x)Q(x)$, where $I(x)$ is an integrating factor."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088140 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e3f6e10-226f-5c83-9188-b6a6922b127a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + \\frac{C}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085469} {:answer/id #uuid "a385077a-3e75-5d98-9292-05e3aaf418e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + \\frac{C}{x^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085470} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8358e2-16cf-59d5-91d5-a818b69196ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x^{2} + \\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085471} {:answer/id #uuid "6a3c1544-df16-5966-b9b5-ab1d3c4d2873" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x + \\frac{C}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085472}] :answer-field/id #uuid "439f959b-4605-586a-bd20-f105125c6174" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id